! mcp_bootstrap_p123.strap -- the K GATE unit-release / queue-entry
! path and the K COMM interrupt-type dispatch, printed page 123.
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.130, header READ and not assumed:
! "TIME CLOCK 00054041  PUNID 11D11MCP  CARD ID EB000049
!  DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER  123", and the header's own
! "LOCATION 215746" names the page's first word.
!
! FIFTY LINES, ALL CODE, NO COMMENT CARDS.  The card-serial column
! runs EB000049 (line 1) to EB000098 (line 50) with NO repeats and
! NO gaps -- one card per line, counted off the serial column, so
! nothing fell off the top or the bottom of the render.  Every one
! of the fifty rows carries a LOCATION and compiles an instruction:
! there is no SYN, no DD, no DRZ, no CNOP and no comment card on
! this page.  That is why all fifty rows are !CHECK'd below.
!
! >>> THE ORIGIN IS NOT SET ON THIS PAGE. <<<  There is no SLC row.
! The SLC written below is the arriving counter, restated so the
! deck assembles standalone, and it is CORROBORATED ON BOTH SIDES:
!   BACKWARD.  printed p.122 is not transcribed yet, but its last
!   two rows are legible in docs/ocr/MCP_Mar63_001-249.txt:
!     "44* 215743.00  000000.72 8B 215746.74 00  BZB,S WR($11),K GATE"
!     "46* 215746.00  216316.32 02             RBXE,C ARC AD"
!   line 46 is a HALF word at 215746.00, so the counter arrives here
!   at 215746.40, which is this page's line 1; and line 44's branch
!   target 215746.74 (0o74, bit 18 SET) is 215746.40 -- p.122 names
!   this page's first word K GATE before this page prints it.
!   FORWARD.  PDF p.131 (printed p.124) prints "LOCATION 216013.00"
!   in its own header, and line 50 here is a half word at
!   216012.40, so the counter leaves at 216013.00.  Two-sided.
!
! THE ADDRESS CHAIN CLOSES END TO END OVER ALL FIFTY ROWS.  FIRST
! printed address 215746.40 (line 1), LAST 216012.40 (line 50);
! every row's printed address plus its own length equals the next
! row's printed address, with no gap and no overlap, across all
! fifty.  That is also an independent check of WHICH rows print one
! half word and which print two -- a single misread there would
! break the chain, and it does not break.
!
! TAIL SCOPE.  printed p.99 line 11's "SLC,(8)215000." moved the
! origin here, and pp.99+ are OUTSIDE the TAIL,(2)G block that
! printed p.2 opened and printed p.98 line 37 closed.  The nine
! names this page DEFINES are all K-prefixed (K GATE, JK GATE,
! K K REL, K Q IN, K LEVEL, K NORM, K COMM, K NINTY, K INT TY) and
! none of them collides with anything in pp.2-98.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE CONTAINS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! One straight-line routine in three parts.
!
! Lines 1-11   K GATE, the unit-status gate.  Line 1 branches round
!              the status-save when the unit is suppressed
!              (BZB,S UN SUP($11),JK GATE); lines 2-4 load the five
!              I/O indicator bits, connect them with C0111 against
!              $7.37, and load a count; line 5 JK GATE stores the
!              count into word 1 of the $13 table; lines 6-11 reset
!              the channel-operating bit, test S MULTI to pick
!              SINGLE-unit (line 8, "-RESET UNIT STA BITS - SINGLE
!              U C") or MULTI-unit (line 11, "-RESET UNIT ST BITS -
!              MULTI U C") clearing, and fall to K K REL.
! Lines 12-13  K K REL, "-CHECK IF RELEASED": BBZ on S REL to W 23K
!              (the fake-EOP exit on printed p.131), BBZ on S SEOP
!              to K FREE.
! Lines 14-20  K Q IN, the queue-entry path: point $9 at S PROG S,
!              take the queue count, store the status bits "-STATUS
!              BITS TO CCW IN FILE A" (line 16), merge five bits
!              into S CCW+.19 with CM0101, step the count, compare
!              against 127 and branch to C Q2BIG if the queue is
!              over-long.
! Lines 21-31  the enqueue proper: index the cumulative queue
!              S CUM Q, set S UN SUP, save $13, test S OWNER with
!              C0011, bump $9 past the owner word (lines 25-26),
!              K LEVEL stores the level bit S M and the 19-bit
!              entry with a progressive index (line 28), restores
!              S CUM Q, adds the 18-bit queue key, and returns
!              through $1.
! Lines 32-34  K NORM: load count 30, KF-compare S L against S M
!              ("-IS SM = SL") and BAL to K SUPRS.
! Lines 35-41  the interrupt entry: save the stored IC S TIC into
!              S INT AD, BAH to C MINTP ("-MCP INTERRUPTING PP"),
!              BZXVZ to C WAIT ("-BRANCH IF WAITING"), and two BB
!              exits to K SUPP on S AS and S SIO.
! Lines 42-46  K COMM, "-BRANCH IF SM IS MCP": pick the PP level
!              register list S LR PP or the MCP one S LR MCP, move
!              fifteen words from S TLR into it, and exit to
!              K UNSTC either way; K NINTY is the second of those
!              exits and carries "-NOT FOUND RETURN".
! Lines 47-50  K INT TY, the interrupt-type tail: load the table of
!              exits location, set S AS, load the IOD reference
!              number, and step $9 back to S PROG S.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE THIRTY-THREE SYMBOLS THIS PAGE REFERENCES AND DOES NOT DEFINE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! EVERY SYN VALUE BELOW IS BACK-DERIVED FROM THIS PAGE'S OWN BINARY
! OUTPUT, so those bits are NOT an independent check of the value --
! the !CHECK rows that use them verify the opcode and modifier bits
! and nothing more.  What makes them trustworthy is the SECOND
! reading given for each one.  Three groups:
!
! (a) SPELLING AND VALUE BOTH TAKEN FROM A LANDED DECK, which read
!     the DEFINING card on its own page.  The value this page's
!     binary implies agrees with the landed one in every case; where
!     the landed deck writes a DECIMAL bit offset it prints octal
!     here, as the rule says (0.30 -> .36, 1.09 -> .11, .25 -> .31):
!       SUNSUP 0.30   p.99 "-UNIT SUPPRESSION"        line 1 .36 ok
!       SIOIND 1.09   p.102 "-IO INDICATORS"          line 2 .11 ok
!       SCHOP  0.26   p.99 "-CHAN OPERATING"          line 6 .32 ok
!       SMULTI 0.27   p.99 "-MULTI-UNIT CHAN"         line 7 .33 ok
!       SRD    0.57   p.99 "-READ, OPERATION STATUS"  line 8 .71 ok
!       SIMNT  0.53   p.70/75/79/97/98/99             line 10 .65 ok
!       SMOUNT 0.51   p.70/75/79/97/98/99 "-MOUNT TAPE" line 11 ok
!       SREL   0.61   p.99 "-RELEASE - OPERATION"     line 12 .75 ok
!       SSEOP  0.46   p.99 "-SUPP END OF OP STATUS"   line 13 .56 ok
!       SAS    .25    p.109 "-AUTO STACK STATUS"      lines 40,48 ok
!       SSIO   .61    p.109 "-SUPPRESSED MODE STATUS" line 41 .75 ok
!       SOWNER 0.32   p.99 "-PRIORITY LEVEL"          line 24 .40 ok
!       SQK    .32    p.109                           lines 15,18,30
!       SCCW   4.0    p.102 "-COPIED CONTROL WORD"    line 17 ok
!       SINTAD 1.32   p.102 "-INTERRUPTED ADDRESS"    line 36 .41 ok
!       STOELO 2.0    p.102 "-TABLE OF EXITS LOCATION" line 47 ok
!       SIODRN 0.0    p.102 "-IOD REFERENCE NUMBER"   line 49 ok
!       SM     SCOMRG+0.05  p.103 "-LEVEL OF INTERRUPT"  lines 27,42
!       SL     SCOMRG+0.04  p.103 "-LEVEL OF INSTRUCTION" line 33
!       SPROGS 215021.0  p.109/117/118                lines 14,50
!       STLR   215230.0  p.116/117/118                lines 43,45
!       STIC   215265.0  p.110/111/112/116/117/118    line 35
!       SLRPP  215266.0  p.116 line 18 DEFINES it     line 43
!       SLRMCP 215362.0  p.116/117                    line 45
!       SCUMQ  215466.0  p.116 line 34 DEFINES it     lines 21,29
!     SLRPP and SCUMQ are the sharpest of these: p.116's own !SYM
!     block gives 215266.00 and 215466.00, and this page's lines
!     43/45 and 21/29 imply exactly those two addresses.
!
! (b) NOT IN ANY LANDED DECK, BUT THE DEFINING CARD IS LEGIBLE IN
!     docs/ocr/MCP_Mar63_001-249.txt AT THE SAME ADDRESS.  The name
!     was read off THIS page and the address off THIS page's binary;
!     the OCR'd row is the independent witness that something with
!     that name is defined there:
!       CMINTP 216215.00  "28* 216215.00 ... C MINTP  C T0C11(BU,5)..."
!       KSUPRS 216223.40  "38* 216223.40  215361.00 30  K SUPRS  LV,0,..."
!       CWAIT  216226.40  "43* 216226.40  000000.31 89 216242.34 02
!                          C WAIT  BB, S AS($9), K SUPP"
!       KFREE  216235.40  "52* 216235.40 ... K FREE  CM0000(BU,1),..."
!       KSUPP  216242.00  "2* 216242.00  216246.70 00  K SUPP  NOP,K COMM"
!       CQ2BIG 216313.00  "216313.00  003410.35 01  C Q2BIG  LVI,$14,SQ2BIG"
!       W23K   220017.40  "14* 220017.40  000040.17 02  W 23K  LCI,7,(8)40
!                          -FAKE EOP"
!       KUNSTC 222037.00  "35* 222037.00  215021.23 01  K UNSTC
!                          LVI,9,S PROG S"
!     W 23K and K UNSTC were the two that looked as though they
!     rested on a branch encoding alone; they do not.  Both defining
!     cards were found by grepping the OCR for the ADDRESS, not the
!     name.
!
! (c) NONE.  There is no external on this page whose value rests on
!     a branch encoding with no second witness.
!
! >>> AND THE BIT OFFSET OF EVERY (8)-FORM SYN ABOVE IS STILL
! UNOBSERVABLE. <<<  All fourteen of them -- CMINTP, KSUPRS, CWAIT,
! KFREE, KSUPP, CQ2BIG, W23K, KUNSTC, SPROGS, STLR, STIC, SLRPP,
! SLRMCP, SCUMQ -- are reached by a branch, an LVI, a T or a
! half-word index op, and bit offsets
! 0-31 all encode identically -- only bit 32, the half-word select,
! shows.  The ".0" and ".40" written below are pinned to the HALF
! WORD and no further; the ".00" is a CONVENTION, and no !CHECK here
! can test it.  (docs/transcription_workflow.md, "WHAT THE ORACLE
! CANNOT SEE", item 1.)
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE NINE NAMES THIS PAGE DEFINES, AND WHAT EACH RESTS ON
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! listing_oracle checks LOCATIONS and BINARY, never SPELLINGS, so
! each name is recorded with its evidence:
!   K GATE   215746.40  CORROBORATED OFF-PAGE.  The OCR prints
!            "B,K GATE" / "B,KGATE" against 215746.50 at three
!            places -- the rows at 216326.40, 216331.00 and
!            217103.00; no PAGE NUMBER header was read for those,
!            so they are cited by ADDRESS and not by page.  printed
!            p.122 line 44 also branches to it.
!            Printed here with NO blanks, "KGATE"; p.122 prints
!            "K GATE".  Same six-or-fewer-character name either way.
!   K COMM   216004.40  CORROBORATED OFF-PAGE: the OCR prints
!            "42* 216226.00  216004.50 00  B,K COMM" and
!            "2* 216242.00 ... K SUPP  NOP,K COMM".
!   K NORM   215776.00  CORROBORATED OFF-PAGE, AND THIS SETTLES A
!            GLYPH.  The name prints here as "K NCRM" -- the third
!            letter is round and open at 1200 dpi.  The OCR shows
!            four other pages referencing 215776: "LVI,$1,KNORM",
!            "LVI,1,K NORM", "B,K NORM" and "BRZ,K NORM -BRANCH IF
!            PP INTERRUPT".  Read K NORM, with an O.  (Dropout can
!            open a closed glyph and never closes an open one.)
!   K NINTY  216010.00  CORROBORATED OFF-PAGE: the OCR prints
!            "38* 222037.40  216010.03 01  LVI,1,K NINTY".  Read at
!            1200 dpi the second letter is an I, not an E -- an E
!            on this drum carries three horizontals and this glyph
!            carries none.  (tesseract reads this page's own name
!            as "K NENTY"; it is wrong.)
!   K INT TY 216010.40  CORROBORATED OFF-PAGE: "B,KINTTY" and
!            "B,K INT TY" against 216010.50, and
!            "BB,S AS($9),K INT TY" against 216010.74.
!   K Q IN   215762.00  CORROBORATED OFF-PAGE: "B,K Q IN" against
!            215762.10 twice and "BZRZ,K Q IN" against 215762.34.
!   JK GATE  215752.00  ON-PAGE ONLY.  Line 1 branches to it and
!            line 5 is at its address, so the ADDRESS is checked;
!            nothing outside this page uses the name, so the
!            SPELLING rests on ink.  Read at 1200 dpi: J K G A T E.
!   K K REL  215760.00  ON-PAGE ONLY, same standing: lines 9 and 10
!            branch to it and line 12 is at its address.
!   K LEVEL  215772.00  ON-PAGE ONLY, same standing: line 25
!            branches to it and line 27 is at its address.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! SUBSTITUTIONS AND SPELLINGS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! (1) BLANKS INSIDE SYMBOL NAMES ARE DROPPED, as on every page since
!     p.2.  Every name here is six or fewer characters once the
!     blanks come out.
! (2) NOTHING IS SUBSTITUTED.  Every printed spelling on this page
!     was handed to strap_asm as printed and accepted, including
!     "KF(BU,1)" (bare KF -- it is "$KF" that is rejected),
!     "C0111", "C0011", "CM0101", "ST(V+I)(BU,19)", "BZXVZ",
!     "$7.37", "$L", "127." and "V-I".  No assembler limitation was
!     hit on this page.
!
! >>> THE 0/C GLYPH PAIR, AND THE ONE BYTE IT NEARLY COST. <<<
! This page opens round glyphs constantly -- its own column heading
! prints "LCCATICN" for LOCATION, and the second octal field of a
! connective prints ".CC" for ".00" on lines 6, 8, 11 and 22 while
! the SIX DIGITS BESIDE IT in the same field print closed zeros.
! So the row-level ink gauge points both ways within one row, and
! every round glyph in a LOCATION or an octal BINARY field is a 0
! with certainty because a C is not an octal digit.
!
! THE BYTE ON LINE 6 IS THE ONE THAT MATTERED.  At 1200 dpi line 6
! reads "001000.CC FC", and tesseract reads it "OCLOOO.CC FC" -- two
! readings, both FC.  IT IS F0, and what settles it is this page's
! OWN SECOND HALF-WORD BYTE COLUMN, not an argument from another
! page:
!   * line 1's byte is "0C" at 1200 dpi, first glyph closed and
!     second open.  Line 1 is "BZB,SUNSUP($11),JKGATE", a branch
!     with no index on the target, so that byte is 00 with
!     certainty -- and it prints its second 0 OPEN.  An open round
!     glyph in this very column is therefore an ordinary 0.
!   * line 12's byte prints "06" with the 0 open, same column.
!   * lines 8 and 11 are the SAME construct as line 6 (CM0000, no
!     "(I')" operand) and their bytes print with the final glyph
!     CLOSED: "F0" and "F0".  An observed 0 is never a damaged C.
!   * the statement is the tie-breaker the workflow names: the low
!     nibble of a VFL/connective full word is i2, the offset index
!     from the OF7(I') operand, and line 6's statement
!     "CM0000(BU,1),S CH OP($10)" has NO second operand, so i2 = 0.
! Written F0 in the !CHECK; the ink reading is recorded here.
!
! >>> AND ONE ROW WHERE THE PRINTED C IS A REAL C, IN THE SAME
! COLUMN. <<<  Line 25's byte is "C2" and it IS 0xC2.  This is the
! only open round glyph on the page that is NOT a zero, and it sits
! in the very column that lines 1, 6, 8, 11 and 12 print zeros into
! with the ink dropped out -- so a transcriber working this column
! on a neighbouring page cannot rely on either default.
! HOW IT WAS SETTLED, and it was not by reading the ink: strap_asm
! emits C2 for "BRZ,K LEVEL" from the opcode alone, with no input
! from this page's print.  The glyph was read as C, the opcode
! produced C, and the two agree; on lines 1, 6, 8 and 11 the same
! two disagreed and the opcode won.  Settle a byte on WHAT PRODUCED
! IT.
!
! >>> B vs 8 ON LINE 18, SETTLED BY THE OPCODE FAMILY. <<<  Line
! 18's first-half byte reads "89" at 600 dpi and the top-right of
! the first glyph has dropped out.  IT IS B9.  At 1800 dpi, beside
! lines 10-13 (which print a clean "8B" each) and line 17 ("8D"),
! the glyph has the straight left stem of a B and not the two round
! bowls of an 8.  Independently: EVERY "V+" index-add row in the
! OCR of this listing prints its byte with the high nibble B --
! "V+,11,-32($12)" at 216026.40 prints "000000.66 BC",
! "V+,$14,S XCHAN" prints "002152.34 B0", and a dozen more.  The
! low nibble 9 is the "($9)" index.  B9.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THREE LISTING MARKS, RECORDED AND NOT EXPLAINED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Lines 5, 20 and 37 print a bare "*" AFTER the location --
! "215752.00 *", "215765.40 *", "216001.00 *" -- where every other
! row prints nothing there.  printed p.103's line 48 and printed
! p.120's lines 6, 11 and 14 carry the same mark.  It changes no
! compiled bit and nothing here checks it.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE CHECKS BELOW DO AND DO NOT ESTABLISH
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! ALL FIFTY ROWS ARE CHECKED; the deck declares no row it did not
! read.  Of those fifty:
!   * FOUR are real checks of address placement, because their
!     operand is a label defined INSIDE this page at an
!     independently printed location: line 1 (-> JK GATE), lines 9
!     and 10 (-> K K REL), and line 25 (-> K LEVEL).
!   * lines 7 and 42 look like two more, but they are NOT: their
!     "$+2.32" is relative to the LOCATION COUNTER, which the SLC
!     below sets by hand, so a wrong origin would move the row and
!     its target together and both rows would still pass.  That is
!     the trap docs/transcription_workflow.md records under "A HARD
!     SLC CANNOT DISAGREE WITH THE ARRIVING COUNTER", measured on
!     printed p.111.  The origin is carried by the fifty-row chain
!     and the two-sided join above, not by these two rows.
!   * the other forty-four carry an external whose value came out
!     of this same print, so they check opcode, length, mode, index
!     and offset bits and NOT the operand address.
! The fifty-row address chain, the two-sided join, and the
! agreement with p.116's and p.109's independently landed values
! are what carry the page.

